examples.solution:
To resolve this issue, you must initialize <code>RSAKeyGenParameterSpec</code> with values that comply with the CRYSL rule. <br><br><b>Compliant Configuration:</b><br><br><ul><li><b>Key size:</b> <code>3072</code> or <code>4096</code></li><li><b>Public exponent:</b> <code>65537</code></li></ul><br><b>Example Fix:</b><br><pre><code>import java.security.spec.RSAKeyGenParameterSpec;
import java.math.BigInteger;

public class SecureRSAConfig {
 public void setup() throws Exception {
 int keySize = 4096;
 BigInteger publicExponent = BigInteger.valueOf(65537);
 RSAKeyGenParameterSpec spec = new RSAKeyGenParameterSpec(keySize, publicExponent);
 // Use this spec with your KeyPairGenerator
}
}</code></pre><br><b>Avoid:</b> Using smaller key sizes like <code>2048</code> or public exponents like <code>3</code>.<br><br>This ensures compliance with modern security recommendations and prevents cryptographic vulnerabilities.

